# November 23, 2026: a third scatter of places across Alberta, the province with the fewest places on the map when this
# session began (46 before). Region ab taken with tools/choose.py --claim --thinnest. Spread from the far north
# (Fort Chipewyan, Bitumount, Bad Heart) through the parkland and Edmonton (Newbrook, Donalda, Dickson, Rossdale, Ritchie Mill)
# to Calgary and Cochrane and the south (Raymond, Cardston, Waterton). Sources opened in this session: the Canadian
# Register of Historic Places statement for each place (historicplaces.ca); Wikipedia as raw text; the Town of Cochrane's
# page; web-search results for hours, which are quoted in the access notes where they were the only source.
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
def L(t,u): return [t,u]
def HP(t,i): return ['Canadian Register of Historic Places: '+t,'https://www.historicplaces.ca/en/rep-reg/place-lieu.aspx?id=%d'%i]
BATCH={'key':'ab_rwqd','label':'Alberta from Fort Chipewyan to Cardston: an oil-sands plant, a straw church, mills and a temple','added':'2026-11-23 23:27'}
PLACES=[
# ---- The far north ----
N('st-pauls-church-fort-chipewyan','Anglican Church of St. Paul the Apostle and the 1874 Day School','Fort Chipewyan','ab','worship','standing',58.7159,-111.1497,'area','view','boat','','1874–1880',
  'Fort Chipewyan began as a fur-trade post in the late 1700s and is counted the first European settlement in Alberta. The Anglican mission started its permanent work there in 1874 with a day school, and the church was finished six years later and consecrated on Easter Sunday 1880 by Bishop William Bompas. It was the first church of the Anglican Diocese of Athabasca and served as the pro-cathedral of the Diocese of Mackenzie from 1912 until 1926. The Register calls it the longest-serving Anglican church in Alberta.',
  'Two small log buildings, a church and the school, with a cemetery between them. Both are hand-hewn logs in post-and-groove construction under white horizontal drop siding. The church is Carpenter Gothic, with a high-pitched roof, an inset spire with a bell and pointed-arch stained-glass windows. The Register says they are the only fur-trade-period buildings left in the community. Whether the church is open to visitors, and when, was not checked: interior not checked.',
  'The oldest Anglican church in Alberta, still among the log houses of a fur-trade post.',
  'On a residential lot in the hamlet of Fort Chipewyan, on the north shore of Lake Athabasca. There is no year-round road from the south: the hamlet is reached by air, by the winter ice road, or by boat, so plan the trip first. Who owns the buildings now was not checked. Look from the street and ask in the community before going near the cemetery. The pin is the Register\'s position.',
  [HP('1880 Anglican Church of St. Paul the Apostle and the 1874 Day School',9088)],
  conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('bitumount-oil-sands-plant','Bitumount: the early oil-sands plants','near Fort McMurray','ab','industry','ruins',57.3862,-111.6449,'site','view','boat','','1925–1958',
  'Robert Fitzsimmons set up the International Bitumen Company here on the Athabasca River in 1925 and built a hot-water separation plant by about 1930, working from the experiments of the scientist Karl Clark. The company was sold in 1943 and renamed Oil Sands Limited. In 1948 the Alberta government took over and built a pilot plant on Clark\'s process, and other companies later ran their own tests on the site. The site was abandoned in the late 1950s. The Register says its work helped lead to the later Suncor and Syncrude plants. It was made a Provincial Historic Resource in 1974.',
  'The Register lists the remains of several clusters: two separation plants with their boiler house, tall stack, storage tanks and fractionating towers, the government pilot plant with its settling and surge tanks and loading ramps, and a camp of houses, cabins, sheds, a root cellar and a sawmill, with old mine pits and tailings nearby. Wikipedia says the buildings were deteriorating as of 2017. What is standing now was not checked.',
  'Where the oil sands were first made to pay, left standing on the river.',
  'About 90 km north of Fort McMurray on the east bank of the Athabasca River, with no public road: a government history site says there were no roads to Bitumount and describes it as reached by river and air. Whether visitors are let in, and who guides them, could not be found. A sign on the site, photographed in 2012, reads "No unauthorized entry" and the site is fenced, so it is closed: ask Alberta Culture\'s Historic Resources Management Branch (Northern Historic Sites, Fort McMurray) before any visit. The pin is the Register\'s position.',
  [HP('Bitumount Site',4998),W('Bitumount'),L('Government of Alberta: Bitumount (history site)','https://history.alberta.ca/energyheritage/bitumount/')],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('bad-heart-straw-church','Bad Heart Straw Church','Teepee Creek','ab','worship','standing',55.485,-118.288,'area','view','drive','','1954',
  'The Roman Catholic community of Bad Heart had no church from its founding in the 1920s until the mid-1950s. Father Francis Dales, a Redemptorist priest working at Sexsmith, more than 50 km away, built one out of straw bales because the people had little money. A concrete slab was laid, pointed salvaged pipes were set in it every three feet, bales were pressed down over the pipes and capped with metal plates, and the walls were stuccoed. All the labour was volunteer and the fittings came from other churches. It was finished in 1954 and the Register says it may be the only church built of straw in Canada.',
  'An L-shaped building with a gable roof, a central steeple and a bank of windows in the front gable; the sides have no windows. The Register says smashed 7-Up bottles were pressed into the rough stucco to give it colour and texture. It stands on a hilltop above the Bad Heart River. Interior not checked, and whether it is still used was not checked.',
  'A church built of straw bales and bottle glass by volunteers.',
  'About 50 km north-east of Grande Prairie, near Teepee Creek, Grande Prairie County. Who looks after it now and whether it opens were not checked. Look from the road. The Register\'s position may fall off the building: the pin is for the area.',
  [HP('Bad Heart Straw Church',11819)],
  conf='mixed',brick='no'),
# ---- The parkland ----
N('newbrook-observatory','Newbrook Observatory','Newbrook','ab','landmark','standing',54.3245,-112.955,'site','view','drive','','1951–1970',
  'Canada and the United States agreed in 1946 to work together on meteor observation, and the clear northern sky at Newbrook suited it. This field station of the Dominion Observatory opened in 1952 with a Super-Schmidt meteor camera, one of two used in Canada, and took thousands of photographs until 1957. On the night of October 4, 1957, the Soviet Union announced Sputnik I, and within a week the resident scientist, Art Griffen, had taken the first North American photograph of it. The observatory closed in 1970 when federal astronomy was consolidated.',
  'A small T-shaped observatory with a cupola and a retractable roof, beside a one-and-a-half-storey residence with an attached garage, on 0.82 hectares at the edge of the hamlet. The Register lists the original floors and siding. Who owns it now, and whether the inside can be seen, was not checked.',
  'Where North America first photographed Sputnik.',
  'On the edge of the hamlet of Newbrook, Thorhild County, about 110 km north-east of Edmonton. Ownership and any visiting were not checked: look from the road. The pin is the Register\'s position.',
  [HP('Newbrook Observatory',8809)],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('donalda-creamery','Donalda Creamery','Donalda','ab','industry','standing',52.5861,-112.576,'area','open','drive','','1954–1987',
  'Local farmers formed the Donalda Co-operative Creamery Association in 1937 to take over the village creamery begun in 1924. In 1954 a machine shop from across the street was moved onto the lot and turned into a new creamery. The co-op stayed independent when the Central Alberta Dairy Pool tried to absorb it, and it closed in 1987 as one of the last farmer-owned creameries in Alberta.',
  'A wood-frame one-and-a-half-storey building with a boomtown front, two small cupolas and a shed addition with a stone chimney. The Register says it still holds dairy equipment from the 1920s to the 1980s: a Babcock tester, a cream tester, a centrifuge, a can washer and three churns from the 1940s, 1950s and 1960s. A search result says the creamery is open for guided tours through the Donalda and District Museum; its own page could not be read, so hours are not given.',
  'A farmers\' creamery with its churns still inside.',
  'On Foster Street, half a block west of the old rail line in the village of Donalda, Stettler County. The Donalda and District Museum (5001 Main Street, 403-883-2100) is said to arrange tours; its page could not be opened and the hours found in search results disagree, so call ahead.',
  [HP('Donalda Creamery',16522),L('Central Museums Alberta: Donalda and District Museum','https://centralmuseumsab.ca/donalda-and-district-museum')],
  conf='mixed',brick='no',hours='?'),
N('dickson-store-museum','Dickson Store and Site','Dickson','ab','landmark','standing',52.0574,-114.311,'site','open','drive','','1909–1947',
  'Carl Christiansen and 17 other Danes settled west of Innisfail in 1903, and in 1909 he opened a general store in what was then called Dickson. It became the centre of the Danish settlement: it sold lumber and dry goods, bought eggs, butter and meat, housed the post office, and from 1919 had an ice cooler that made it the local collecting point for the Markerville Creamery. Christiansen added three extensions between 1911 and 1930 and set up some of Alberta\'s earliest rural freezer lockers in 1947.',
  'A two-storey L-shaped wood-frame store, a garage and a lumber shed, with a lawn, flagpole and garden. Run today as the Dickson Store Museum, a seasonal museum. What is on show inside was not checked.',
  'A Danish general store kept as it was.',
  'In the hamlet of Dickson, Red Deer County, 3.2 km south of Spruce View and 29 km west of Innisfail on Highway 54. Search results call it a seasonal museum, from mid-May to Labour Day, but two listings gave different days and times (one 10 a.m. to 5:30 p.m. daily, one Monday to Saturday 10 to 5 and Sunday 11 to 5), and its own site could not be read: call 403-728-3355 before going.',
  [HP('Dickson Store and Site',6557),L('Central Museums Alberta: Dickson Store Museum','https://centralmuseumsab.ca/dickson-store-museum')],
  conf='mixed',brick='no',hours='?'),
# ---- Edmonton ----
N('rossdale-power-plant','Rossdale Power Plant','Edmonton','ab','industry','standing',53.52931,-113.49861,'site','view','drive','','1902–2012',
  'Electricity has been made on this river bank since 1902, when Edmonton bought the first power station for $13,000. The Low Pressure Plant, designed by Maxwell Dewar, was begun in 1931 and grew in six stages into the 1950s; Pumphouse No. 1 followed in 1937, and Depression relief workers did part of the building. The plant stopped making power in 2012 and is owned by EPCOR. Alberta made it a Provincial Historic Resource in 2001, ending a plan for a new gas turbine, and in 2013 city council voted $3 million to stabilize it over ten years.',
  'Three long flat-roofed blocks of different heights running down to the river, with steel frames, brick and glass-block walls, white precast bands carrying the date of each stage, and a row of tall stacks. The Register calls it the only power plant of its scale and period left in Alberta. Interior walking tours were reported from August 2020; whether they run now was not checked.',
  'Edmonton\'s river-bank power plant, shut and waiting for a new use.',
  'On the north bank of the North Saskatchewan River at 10155 96 Avenue in Rossdale, beside the Walterdale Bridge approach. EPCOR owns the site. Look from the river valley paths and the street; no hours are given because none were found.',
  [HP('Rossdale Power Plant',5896),W('Rossdale Power Plant')],
  conf='mixed',brick='some'),
N('ritchie-mill-edmonton','Ritchie Mill (North West Mill and Feed Company)','Edmonton','ab','industry','standing',53.52344,-113.49433,'site','view','drive','','1892–1948',
  'Robert Ritchie built this flour mill in 1892 at the end of steel, where the Calgary and Edmonton Railway stopped at the south side of the river in Strathcona. It used steam-powered steel rollers, not the stone wheels that pitted on hard prairie wheat, and elevators were added in 1895 and 1902 (another source says 1905). It milled flour until 1948. The Register calls it the oldest surviving flour mill in Alberta.',
  'Several linked blocks of two to four storeys on the south escarpment of the river valley, looking across to downtown: the original three-storey mill of 1892 to 1893, a blind four-storey elevator, a partly brick four-storey north building and a two-storey west addition. It now houses offices and shops, and search results list it as a private office building, so the interior is not open to sightseers.',
  'Alberta\'s oldest flour mill, now offices.',
  'At 10170 Saskatchewan Drive in Old Strathcona, Edmonton, on the corner of Tommy Banks Way. A Provincial Historic Resource since 1979. Look from the street and the river-valley path; street parking nearby.',
  [HP('Ritchie Mill (North West Mill and Feed Company)',5722),W('Ritchie Mill')],
  brick='some'),
# ---- Calgary and Cochrane ----
N('calgary-city-hall-old','Calgary City Hall (Old City Hall)','Calgary','ab','civic','standing',51.046,-114.0574,'exact','view','drive','','1907–1911',
  'Built from 1907 to 1911 on the site of a wood-frame town hall, to a design by William Dodd, as a statement of what Calgary expected to become. It first held the mayor and aldermen, city offices, police, law courts and the city telephone system, and was too small by 1913. Robert Borden opened it in June 1911. The Register calls it the oldest surviving city hall in Alberta.',
  'A four-storey Richardsonian Romanesque building of rough local sandstone on a steel frame, with a single clock tower, twin stone verandas, a deeply recessed front door between two red granite columns, arched windows with keystones, and a red pressed-metal tile roof with a glass dome. A red granite datestone bears 1907. The Register says the inside has been much altered. It still holds the mayor\'s and councillors\' offices.',
  'The sandstone city hall of a boom town.',
  'At 700 Macleod Trail SE in downtown Calgary, beside the 1985 Municipal Building. Active council offices: look from the plaza. Whether the public may go in, and when, was not checked: interior not checked.',
  [HP('Calgary City Hall',8981),W('Calgary City Hall')],
  brick='no'),
N('cochrane-ranche-site','Cochrane Ranche historic site','Cochrane','ab','landmark','traces',51.1990,-114.4789,'area','public','drive','','1881–1919',
  'The Cochrane Ranche Company took the first large grazing lease in the North-West Territories in 1881, 109,000 acres west of Calgary, and began building beside Big Hill Creek under its first manager, the retired police superintendent James Walker. Hard winters killed many cattle, and in 1883 the herds were moved south to the Belly River. The land was sold in 1888. Two brickyards worked on the property between 1898 and 1919, supplying Calgary\'s building boom. It was made a Provincial Historic Resource in 1976.',
  'The Register lists the buried remains of the manager\'s house, bunkhouses, stable, blacksmith shop, dugout, brickyards and corrals, with a quarry and the creek valley, which a visitor will not see as buildings. The Town of Cochrane describes the site as a public park of about 55 hectares with an outdoor stage, picnic tables, fire pits and seasonal washrooms, and a statue of a man on a horse, the Men of Vision.',
  'Alberta\'s first big ranch, with a park on the foundations.',
  'The Town of Cochrane lists it among its facilities, at 80 Highway 1A, east of Highway 22; hours and parking were not on the page. The pin marks the park in general: no lot was found.',
  [HP('Cochrane Ranche',11541),L('Town of Cochrane: Cochrane Ranche Historic Site','https://www.cochrane.ca/node/1209')],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
# ---- The south ----
N('raymond-buddhist-church','Raymond Buddhist Church','Raymond','ab','worship','standing',49.4605,-112.662,'exact','view','drive','','1902–1903',
  'Latter-day Saint settlers built it in 1902 to 1903 as the first schoolhouse in Raymond, and it served as their meeting house too until the school moved in 1910 and the church built its Park Avenue Chapel in 1929. That year Japanese settlers, mostly Jodo Shinshu Buddhists, bought it for a temple, school and meeting place, and a co-op store, the Kobai Kumiai, ran in it until the 1990s. The Register calls it the only original building left from Raymond\'s founding. The Buddhist congregation moved to Lethbridge in 2006, taking most of the interior fittings.',
  'A grey two-storey wooden building with white trim, horizontal siding, a steeply pitched gable roof and a truncated tower carrying a decorative plaque. A Buddhist altar was given to it in 1946; the Register says most of the interior decoration left in 2006. What the building is used for now was not checked.',
  'A pioneer schoolhouse that became a Buddhist temple.',
  'At 35 Broadway Avenue South in Raymond. Ownership and use today were not checked; look from the street. Provincially recognized since 1984.',
  [HP('Raymond Buddhist Church',8773),W('Raymond Buddhist Church')],
  conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('cardston-alberta-temple','Cardston Alberta Temple','Cardston','ab','worship','standing',49.19826,-113.30932,'exact','view','drive','','1913–1923',
  'Cardston was Canada\'s first Mormon settlement, founded in 1887. Work on the temple began on November 9, 1913 and the building was consecrated in 1923. It was designed by the Salt Lake City architects Hyrum Pope and Harold Burton. It was named a National Historic Site in 1992.',
  'A granite building rising in a stepped pyramid on a small hill in the middle of town, with strong horizontal lines in the manner of Frank Lloyd Wright\'s Prairie School and a stone wall round the grounds, framed by Chief Mountain. The Register praises the inlaid and panelled hardwoods and light fittings inside. The inside is closed to the public: once a temple is dedicated only church members with a current recommend may enter (it was open to everyone before its dedication).',
  'A granite Prairie School temple in a ranching town.',
  'At 348 Third Street West, Cardston, on grounds that can be walked round. Look at the outside; the church\'s own page for the grounds and any visitor centre was not checked.',
  [HP('Temple of the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter Day Saints',12645),W('Cardston Alberta Temple')],
  brick='no'),
N('first-oil-well-waterton','First Oil Well in Western Canada','Waterton Park','ab','industry','marker',49.071,-113.987,'site','public','drive','','1901–1904',
  'John Lineham of the Rocky Mountain Development Company struck oil here in 1902 at 1,024 feet after drilling since 1901. The well was small and ran dry in 1904, but it showed that oil lay under the foothills and led to the larger fields found later. It became a National Historic Site in 1965.',
  'A well-head opening and drill stem capped by a monument in the shape of an oil derrick, put up in 1968. Most of the visible remains of the drilling were removed then; the Register says archaeological remains lie within 15 feet of the well-head, and old mining refuse, an oil pool, a grave and telegraph poles are nearby.',
  'The little well that started Western Canada\'s oil.',
  'On the Akamina Parkway in Waterton Lakes National Park, between Cameron Creek and about mile 5, east of the townsite. Run by Parks Canada; the park\'s fees and the road\'s seasons were not checked.',
  [HP('First Oil Well in Western Canada National Historic Site',4210)],
  conf='mixed',brick='no'),
]
